# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Demonstrator for the DRAWN annotation, `note=` (MIGRATIONS 0.3).
# It exercises all four acceptor kinds in one figure: a note on a `node`, on a
# `group`, on an `edge`, and the figure-level note on `title`.
#
# `note=` is not `description=`. The two keys divide by AUDIENCE: `description=`
# reaches the reading agent as an SVG <title> and puts no ink on the page, while
# `note=` is an explanation the human must SEE. Neither is a fallback for the
# other, and writing both on one element is legal.
#
# The author never places the box (`DOMAIN-CONVENTION-DIRECTIVES`): there is no at=, no side=, no
# left of/right of. The engine chooses adjacency where it can and reaches for a
# leader line only when adjacency fails.
figdown 0.3 block

# The figure-level note — `title`'s first option key. It carries the class of
# annotation that names no single element, which is 14% of the measured demand.
title "Ingress datapath — pause/PFC handling" note="Rates are per port, not per lane. Link speed is fixed at 25G."

class dropped "Frames discarded here" stroke=#b91c1c

external wire "from the wire"

# A note on a GROUP — the largest single class of measured targets. Half the
# demand annotates a region rather than a box.
group mac "MAC layer" fill=transparent style=dashed note="Runs on the recovered RX clock, not the core clock."
node rx "RX ring" shape=box in=mac
node fcs "FCS check" shape=rounded in=mac

# A note on a NODE.
node pause "Pause / PFC" shape=diamond note="Only valid while the port is up."
node fwd "Forward" shape=rounded
node drop "Discard" shape=box class=dropped

edge wire [eth0] -> rx
edge rx -> fcs
# A note on an EDGE. This is the acceptor that decides the shape: a connector
# has no id, so an attribute is the only form that can reach it at all.
edge fcs -> pause note="250 MHz, 64b datapath"
edge pause -[pass]-> fwd
edge pause -[assert]-> drop

flow right
